<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bardiya</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 522 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> · Also known as Smerdis, Tanyoxarces, Bardia, Gaumata</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bardiya, called Smerdis by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>, was a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> and the younger brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambyses-ii">Cambyses II</a>, both Persian kings. Views of him are sharply divided: either he ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> for a few months in 522 BCE, or he had been secretly killed by Cambyses and was impersonated by a magus called Gaumata. The second version is that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius the Great</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/behistun-inscription">Behistun Inscription</a>, Herodotus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesias">Ctesias</a>, in which seven nobles killed the ruler in September 522 BCE and Darius took the throne. Most modern historians assume that the man who ruled was the real Bardiya and that Darius invented the impostor to justify his seizure of the throne. Bardiya's only daughter, Parmys, married Darius.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bardiya.jpg" alt="Bardiya" width="330" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artist Unknown artist ⧼for⧽ Darius I · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bisotun_Iran_Relief_Achamenid_Period.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Six of them descended from the nobles who joined Darius in 522 BCE to kill the man he called the usurper <b>Gaumata</b>: Otanes, Intaphernes, Gobryas, Hydarnes, Megabyzus and Aspathines.</blockquote><a href="/societies/achaemenid-persia">Social Hierarchy of the Achaemenid Empire</a></li></ul></section>
